  "solution": {
    "text": "The electronegativity order of hybridized carbons is <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> . So in an allylic halide, such as <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> , the alpha carbon is much more partially positive than the alpha carbon in <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> . Therefore, the attacking nucleophile will be attracted to the alpha carbon more in the allylic halide compound than in the primary halide. Hence, allylic halides tend to follow <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>S</mtext> <mtext>N</mtext> </msub> <mtext>2</mtext></mrow> </math> pathways more than primary halides.<br />",
    "image": null
  },
